WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.201
C requires developers to explicitly
allocate and release dynamic memory.

2
00:00:04.281 --> 00:00:08.394
Functions such as malloc and calloc create
allocations that remain reserved until

3
00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:09.884
they are released with free.

4
00:00:09.964 --> 00:00:13.430
The compiler does not infer when a heap
allocation is no longer needed,

5
00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:17.681
and the language does not automatically
attach cleanup responsibility to a pointer

6
00:00:17.761 --> 00:00:18.290
variable.

7
00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:21.777
The pointer returned from an allocation
function is only an address.

8
00:00:21.857 --> 00:00:24.031
It is not, by itself, an ownership
contract.

9
00:00:24.111 --> 00:00:27.753
The same pointer type can represent an
owned allocation, a borrowed view,

10
00:00:27.833 --> 00:00:30.359
an element inside a larger object, a
global object,

11
00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:33.377
or a pointer returned by a library with
its own cleanup rule.

12
00:00:33.457 --> 00:00:37.040
The ownership meaning comes from the API
contract and surrounding code.

13
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.234
Ownership describes who is responsible for
releasing memory.

14
00:00:40.314 --> 00:00:43.369
A function that allocates may return
ownership to its caller.

15
00:00:43.449 --> 00:00:45.211
A structure may own a nested buffer.

16
00:00:45.291 --> 00:00:49.463
A container may own its elements, or it
may only reference elements owned elsewhere.

17
00:00:49.543 --> 00:00:53.714
Reviewers should be able to identify the
owner without searching through unrelated

18
00:00:53.794 --> 00:00:54.381
code paths.

19
00:00:54.461 --> 00:00:57.869
Borrowed pointers provide access without
cleanup responsibility.

20
00:00:57.949 --> 00:01:00.886
A borrowed pointer may be valid only
during a function call,

21
00:01:00.966 --> 00:01:04.256
while a parent object remains alive, or
until a container changes.

22
00:01:04.336 --> 00:01:07.508
Borrowing is useful, but it must not be
confused with ownership.

23
00:01:07.588 --> 00:01:11.407
Code that borrows a pointer should not
free it unless the contract explicitly

24
00:01:11.487 --> 00:01:12.603
transfers ownership.

25
00:01:12.683 --> 00:01:15.679
Every allocation should have a clear
expected cleanup path.

26
00:01:15.759 --> 00:01:18.638
That cleanup path may be a direct free, a
destroy function,

27
00:01:18.718 --> 00:01:21.891
a cleanup helper for a structure, a
container teardown routine,

28
00:01:21.971 --> 00:01:23.557
or a caller-owned release step.

29
00:01:23.637 --> 00:01:27.044
If no one can name the cleanup path when
the allocation is introduced,

30
00:01:27.124 --> 00:01:28.887
the code is already harder to trust.

31
00:01:28.967 --> 00:01:30.788
Good ownership design is practical.

32
00:01:30.868 --> 00:01:34.628
It reduces leaks, prevents repeated
cleanup, and makes maintenance safer.

33
00:01:34.708 --> 00:01:37.116
When an allocation crosses a function
boundary,

34
00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:39.840
the contract should say who owns it, who
may borrow it,

35
00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.740
how long it remains valid, and which
routine releases it.
